{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3c05daf3b1c118b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\tTriage: neither below-threshold nor docs-only. The strategy is Critical-priority\n13\tcompliance work spanning multiple architecturally distinct work streams (image\n14\tconversion in two components, a compliance/security process with human gates,\n15\tand substantive documentation), so it proceeds through full decomposition.\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n20\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | KServe serving-stack image FIPS 140-3 conversion | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | Platform Operator FIPS 140-3 conversion | Implementation | RHOAI Platform (assumed — see Health Warnings) | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | FIPS support posture documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving (assumed — see Health Warnings) | P0 |\n25\t\n26\tNote on E004's priority: per the docs-authoring priority exception, docs epics\n27\tderive priority from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Critical` → P0), not\n28\tfrom HLR-4's P2 label, and do not push priority upstream.\n29\t\n30\t## Dependency DAG\n31\t\n32\t```mermaid\n33\tgraph TD\n34\t    E001[\"E001 KServe FIPS conversion\"]\n35\t    E002[\"E002 Platform Operator FIPS conversion\"]\n36\t    E003[\"E003 Compliance evidence package\"]\n37\t    E004[\"E004 FIPS documentation\"]\n38\t    E001 --> E003\n39\t    E002 --> E003\n40\t    E001 --> E004\n41\t    E002 --> E004\n42\t    E003 --> E004\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\t## DAG Justification\n46\t\n47\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n48\t|------|------|-----------|\n49\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The evidence package aggregates the module inventory, build attestations, and test results the KServe conversion produces; it cannot be assembled before those artifacts exist. |\n50\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | Same — the evidence package consumes the Platform Operator conversion's inventory, attestations, and test results. |\n51\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must describe the implemented FIPS posture; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n52\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n53\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — the evidence epic is an Implementation epic, and the documentation's support statements must reflect the assembled compliance posture, not contradict it. |\n54\t\n55\tNon-edges (parallel / merged work):\n56\t\n57\t- **E001 ∥ E002** — different components in different repos with no shared\n58\t  artifacts (Rule 14); both conversions run in parallel.\n59\t- **HLR-3 (CI FIPS regression check) is not a standalone epic.** It is a\n60\t  shared-surface enforcement deliverable over the same build surfaces being\n61\t  converted. Per the per-surface division rule, each conversion epic delivers\n62\t  and verifies the gating check for its own component's builds; a standalone\n63\t  CI epic would carve one logical change into a work-type epic and leave each\n64\t  conversion epic's slice incomplete.\n65\t- **HLR-2's module inventory is likewise divided per surface**: each\n66\t  conversion epic produces the crypto module inventory for its own images, and\n67\t  the evidence epic aggregates. No standalone inventory epic.\n68\t- **No Investigation epics.** The strategy's Open Questions section states\n69\t  \"None on scope\"; no risks table, assumptions, conditional ADRs, or pending\n70\t  reviews exist. The externally controlled lab/review-board timing affects the\n71\t  schedule, not which epics exist or what they do, so it is a risk note on the\n72\t  evidence epic, not an unknown requiring resolution work.\n73\t- **No konflux-onboarding / repo-onboarding / license-validation epics.** KServe\n74\t  is already Konflux-onboarded (architecture context). Platform Operator is an\n75\t  existing canonical component whose images the strategy treats as existing;\n76\t  see Health Warnings for the missing-context caveat.\n77\t\n78\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n79\t\n80\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n81\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n82\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 (KServe images), E002 (Platform Operator images) | Split by component boundary (Rule 1); each epic covers its component's serving-stack images. |\n83\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E003 (aggregation + submission), with inventory/attestation/test-result slices in E001 and E002 | E003 assembles and submits the package; the per-image inputs are produced by the conversion epics (per-surface division). |\n84\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E001, E002 (bundled) | Bundled: the CI gating check enforces the very build surfaces the P0 conversion work converts — it shares the build/pipeline surface with the P0 work and is not independently deferrable; splitting it would produce a standalone work-type epic plus a second wiring pass over the same pipelines. Per the shared-surface rule, the check's slice is delivered and verified inside each conversion epic. |\n85\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Standalone docs-authoring epic (substantive new doc content, per the work-product-type boundary). Priority P0 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Critical → P0). |\n86\t\n87\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n88\t\n89\t## Health Warnings\n90\t\n91\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n92\t   Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), no\n93\t   Non-Functional Requirements, and no Technical Approach / Staff Engineer\n94\t   Input. The decomposition proceeded from the HLRs and Affected Components;\n95\t   per-epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR text and the\n96\t   implementation rules.\n97\t2. **Team assignments are assumptions.** KServe's owner (RHOAI Serving) comes\n98\t   from the architecture context (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`). The\n99\t   Platform Operator owner (RHOAI Platform) and the documentation owner (RHOAI\n100\t   Serving) are not stated anywhere in the strategy — confirm before\n101\t   scheduling.\n102\t3. **Scope ambiguity — \"serving data plane\".** The problem statement says\n103\t   \"KServe components and the serving data plane link non-validated crypto\n104\t   libraries\", but Affected Components lists only KServe and Platform\n105\t   Operator. If \"serving data plane\" is intended to include Serving Runtimes\n106\t   images (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit are KServe's model-execution dependencies\n107\t   per architecture context), an additional conversion epic for Serving\n108\t   Runtimes is needed and the evidence epic's scope widens. The strategy\n109\t   asserts no open scope questions, so this is flagged for human verification\n110\t   rather than spun into an Investigation epic. Relatedly, \"several images\" is\n111\t   never enumerated — the per-image audit is a deliverable of each conversion\n112\t   epic.\n113\t4. **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file.** Its maturity,\n114\t   owning team, and Konflux onboarding status cannot be corroborated. It is an\n115\t   existing canonical RHAI component (present in `.context/rhai-components.txt`)\n116\t   and the strategy treats its images as already existing, so no onboarding\n117\t   chain was generated; this missing context is why E002's\n118\t   `architecture_claims` signal is 0. If the Platform Operator build pipeline\n119\t   is not Konflux-onboarded, a `konflux-onboarding` epic may need adding —\n120\t   kept parallel with E002 per Rule 10, never as a dependency edge.\n121\t5. **Externally controlled schedule, deliberately not ACs.** The accredited\n122\t   lab's six-to-nine-month historical turnaround and the security review\n123\t   board's cadence dominate the schedule and cannot be compressed by\n124\t   engineering effort. They are recorded as risks on the evidence epic; that\n125\t   epic's acceptance criteria stop at the engineering completion point the\n126\t   strategy controls (package assembled, submitted through the review board,\n127\t   triaged, delivered to the lab) — the certification decision itself is the\n128\t   lab's, not an AC.\n129\t\n130\t## Tiered Delivery\n131\t\n132\tNot applicable as a DAG split. The external party with uncertain timing (the\n133\taccredited certification lab) gates no engineering epic: all conversion, CI,\n134\tand evidence-assembly work proceeds regardless, and Tier-1 value (HLR-1/HLR-3 —\n135\timages running correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters with regression enforcement)\n136\tis delivered by E001/E002 independent of the certification outcome. Only the\n137\tcertification decision itself waits on the lab, and that is outside\n138\tengineering control.\n139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-893f1b092e44f041","agent_id":"a942642b9696894a4","session_id":"c0af6202-03d2-4ad7-8476-de2ac99e02dd","uuid":"927c962e-39b0-4619-b7ed-c24d44d0249c","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:12:42.018Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3c05daf3b1c118b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
