{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-85345ed3d55cb3e6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — epic body\n15\tsections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority\n16\tinheritance, and implementation-type parallelism all pass).\n17\t\n18\tTriage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition) with `epic_count: 4` —\n19\tmulti-epic output is expected; no triage contradiction.\n20\t\n21\tThe decomposition is a faithful, rule-conformant translation of the strategy.\n22\tIt correctly navigated the hard cases this strategy poses: the externally\n23\tcontrolled lab/review-board schedule is kept out of acceptance criteria (recorded\n24\tas risks), the shared-surface CI check and module inventory are divided per\n25\tsurface into the conversion epics rather than extracted as standalone work-type\n26\tepics, the docs-authoring epic derives priority from the strategy's Critical\n27\tpriority per the exception, and the Platform Operator Konflux question is kept\n28\tas a parallel non-edge per Rule 10. No critical or major issues found.\n29\t\n30\t## Criterion Details\n31\t\n32\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n33\t\n34\tAll four HLRs map to at least one epic:\n35\t\n36\t- HLR-1 (P0) → E001 (KServe images) + E002 (Platform Operator images), split by\n37\t  component boundary.\n38\t- HLR-2 (P0) → E003 (aggregation + board submission + lab delivery), with the\n39\t  per-image evidence inputs produced as slices of E001/E002.\n40\t- HLR-3 (P1) → bundled into E001 and E002. This is the correct call under the\n41\t  shared-surface rule: the CI gating check enforces the very build pipelines the\n42\t  P0 conversion work converts, so it is not independently deferrable, and the\n43\t  bundling is recorded with inline rationale in the HLR Traceability Matrix row\n44\t  (`(bundled)` marker with the shared-surface justification) as required.\n45\t- HLR-4 (P2) → E004, a standalone docs-authoring epic; covered, not deferred.\n46\t\n47\tPriority inheritance: E001 and E002 are P0 (HLR-1); E003 (P0) is blocked only by\n48\tP0 epics. E004's P0 is correctly derived from the strategy's `priority: Critical`\n49\tvia the docs-authoring exception, not from HLR-4's P2 label, and it pushes no\n50\tpriority upstream. No priority collapse with deferrable features — the only\n51\tlower-priority HLRs bundled into P0 epics (HLR-3) share the implementation\n52\tsurface with the P0 work, with rationale recorded.\n53\t\n54\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n55\t\n56\tCross-check (diagram ↔ frontmatter, both directions): E003's `dependencies` =\n57\t[E001, E002] and E004's = [E001, E002, E003] exactly match the five diagram\n58\tedges; E001/E002 have empty dependencies matching their parallel roots. Arrows\n59\tare drawn dependency → dependent per the `graph TD` convention. No cycles.\n60\tCritical path length 3 (E001→E003→E004) is within the M-standard heuristic\n61\t(3-4) and matches the summary frontmatter.\n62\t\n63\tEdge justification:\n64\t\n65\t- E001/E002 → E003 (Rule 13, artifact consumption): valid data-dependency\n66\t  edges. The evidence package's substance is the module inventories, build\n67\t  attestations, and FIPS test results produced by the conversion work — the\n68\t  module inventory is an audit deliverable and the test results verify the\n69\t  converted images, so they cannot exist before the conversions. This is a\n70\t  genuine general→general data dependency, not the configuration-reference\n71\t  carve-out (image digests/attestation content consumed to protect\n72\t  konflux-onboarding parallelism under Rule 10): there is no konflux-onboarding\n73\t  epic in this decomposition, and the cross-check completeness rule requires\n74\t  exactly these edges for an epic whose deliverable consumes another epic's\n75\t  outputs.\n76\t- E001/E002/E003 → E004 (Rule 11, docs last): E004 is blocked by all three\n77\t  Implementation epics — complete docs-last coverage, confirmed by the\n78\t  validator. These edges correctly do not trigger priority inheritance.\n79\t\n80\tParallelism: E001 ∥ E002 run parallel (different components/repos, no shared\n81\tartifacts — Rule 14). No `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or\n82\t`license-validation` epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 pairings are not implicated;\n83\tthe Platform Operator onboarding contingency (Health Warning 4) is explicitly\n84\tkept parallel, never an edge. Cross-check completeness: every\n85\tcontent-discernible dependency has an edge (E004's evidence-package consistency\n86\trequirement → E003 edge present; E003's evidence inputs → E001/E002 edges\n87\tpresent). No conditional branch files exist and none are required.\n88\t\n89\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n90\t\n91\tComponent/team tuples are clean: E001 (KServe / RHOAI Serving), E002 (Platform\n92\tOperator / RHOAI Platform), E003 (KServe / RHOAI Serving), E004 (Documentation /\n93\tRHOAI Serving). All component names are canonical (validator-confirmed). No\n94\tepic bundles work across teams; E003's cross-component aggregation is one\n95\tlogical change (a single evidence package and submission) owned by one team,\n96\tcorrectly not split per component.\n97\t\n98\tSize: E001/E002 are one-logical-change sweeps over an enumerated (to-be-audited)\n99\timage set presented by the strategy as one work item — the compliant shape.\n100\tThe conditional re-scope trigger is present (E001 Risks/Notes: \"If the image\n101\taudit materially exceeds the strategy's 'several images' estimate, re-scope\n102\tbefore continuing\"); per the size-check rule no split is demanded. Watch note:\n103\tthe trigger lives in the epic's Risks/Notes rather than the summary's Health\n104\tWarnings (HW3 discusses the enumeration but not the trigger) — acceptable, no\n105\tcorrection demanded.\n106\t\n107\tNo scope duplication: E001 and E002 build disjoint surfaces (different\n108\tcomponents, repos, teams); E003 aggregates rather than rebuilds. No\n109\twork-product mixing: the docs content is a separate docs-authoring epic; the\n110\tinventories/CI checks inside E001/E002 are engineering deliverables intrinsic\n111\tto the conversion, correctly divided per surface per the shared-surface rule\n112\t(no standalone CI or inventory epic). Watch note: E003's frontmatter\n113\t`component: KServe` under-describes its cross-component aggregation scope, but\n114\tthe epic body is explicit about consuming both components' outputs and no\n115\tbetter single canonical value exists — no correction demanded.\n116\t\n117\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n118\t\n119\tAll four epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact\n120\t(converted images + inventories + CI checks; evidence package + submissions;\n121\tpublished documentation). No Investigation epics — correctly so: the strategy's\n122\tOpen Questions section states \"None on scope\", and the externally controlled\n123\tlab/review-board timing affects schedule, not which epics exist or what they\n124\tdo (the informational-only test of Rule 4). The \"serving data plane\" phrase\n125\tambiguity was correctly routed to an ambiguity flag (HW3) with human\n126\tverification rather than an Investigation epic, since the strategy's Affected\n127\tComponents list and \"none on scope\" assertion are the authoritative scope\n128\tsignals. No gates exist, so `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` are uniformly null\n129\t— consistent.\n130\t\n131\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n132\t\n133\tAll four epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (the 9-signal\n134\trubric) with `investigation_signals: null` — type-appropriate. All four\n135\tai-signals files exist, and each rationale matches its frontmatter value:\n136\t\n137\t- E001: signal-by-signal consistent; `architecture_claims: 1` is correctly\n138\t  calibrated — the strategy's premise (KServe as the serving stack linking\n139\t  non-validated crypto) is corroborated by\n140\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`, cited in the\n141\t  rationale.\n142\t- E002: `architecture_claims: 0` with the accurate \"no architecture-context\n143\t  file covers Platform Operator\" rationale — verified: only `kserve.md` exists\n144\t  in the context tree. Flagged as Health Warning 4, consistent.\n145\t- E003: greenfield `existing_foundation: -1` matches the strategy's \"we have no\n146\t  compliance evidence package\"; `external_dependency: -1` and\n147\t  `human_process_gates: -1` match the lab coordination and mandatory review\n148\t  board sign-off.\n149\t- E004: `architecture_claims: 0` rationale (\"no architecture-context file\n150\t  covers the Documentation component\") is factually accurate — verified against\n151\t  the context tree; `human_process_gates: 0` correctly treats technical review\n152\t  as normal docs workflow, and it is an AC.\n153\t\n154\tNo signal value contradicts the rubric conditions or the strategy content.\n155\t\n156\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n157\t\n158\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs are\n159\tpresent: E004 has the technical-review-against-implementation AC (Rule 24);\n160\tE001/E002 each carry a build-pipeline-green AC (Rule 25, belt-and-suspenders\n161\there since no onboarding chain exists). Rule 23 is not triggered — the strategy\n162\thardens crypto modules in images; it does not replace an existing capability.\n163\t\n164\tThe canonical soft-AC trap is correctly avoided: the lab's six-to-nine-month\n165\tturnaround and the review-board cadence are recorded in E003's Risks/Notes and\n166\tHealth Warning 5, not as ACs. E003's ACs stop at the engineering completion\n167\tpoint the strategy controls (package complete with no gaps, board-triaged to\n168\tacceptance, delivered to the lab) — each conditions the epic's own deliverable,\n169\tand the certification decision itself is correctly excluded. No AC states an\n170\texternally controlled precondition. No epic body references sibling epics by\n171\tdraft ID (grep-verified: E00x strings appear only in frontmatter fields).\n172\t\n173\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n174\t\n175\tAll strategy scope is covered: HLR-1 through HLR-4 map to the epic set with no\n176\torphaned requirements; the Out of Scope boundaries (workbenches/pipelines/\n177\ttraining, Common Criteria) are carried into E004's documentation scope so\n178\tsupport statements are unambiguous. Cross-epic consistency holds: the\n179\tper-image inventories/attestations/test results produced by E001/E002 are\n180\texactly what E003 aggregates (\"no gaps against the inventories\"), and E004's\n181\tcontent is kept consistent with the assembled package via the E003 edge.\n182\t\n183\tStrategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the missing strategy\n184\tsections (HW1), team-assignment assumptions (HW2), the \"serving data plane\" /\n185\t\"several images\" scope ambiguity with its concrete consequence — an additional\n186\tServing Runtimes conversion epic if intended (HW3, consistent with kserve.md's\n187\tlisting of vLLM/OpenVINO/Caikit as KServe's model-execution dependencies), the\n188\tPlatform Operator context gap (HW4), and the external schedule (HW5) are all\n189\trecorded. Tiered delivery is correctly analyzed as not applicable: the lab\n190\tgates no engineering epic, and Tier-1 value (HLR-1/HLR-3) ships via E001/E002\n191\tindependent of the certification outcome. The docs-authoring epic is present\n192\tfor the documentation requirement. No conditional branches are needed — the\n193\tstrategy states no open scope questions.\n194\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-808043cf65d3d7ca","agent_id":"aac3cdd69d08a7605","session_id":"c0af6202-03d2-4ad7-8476